 "target": 30.0,
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Farmers in New Zealand had a problem: their pants kept exploding.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.286
  },
  {
   "text": "One pair went bang while the farmer was still wearing them.",
   "s": 3.586,
   "e": 5.942
  },
  {
   "text": "The cause? A weedkiller called sodium chlorate.",
   "s": 6.242,
   "e": 8.063
  },
  {
   "text": "In the 1930s, the government told farmers to spray it on ragwort.",
   "s": 8.363,
   "e": 11.805
  },
  {
   "text": "It killed the weeds. But the spray soaked into their trousers.",
   "s": 12.105,
   "e": 14.663
  },
  {
   "text": "So once the cloth dried, it burned like a match head.",
   "s": 14.963,
   "e": 17.241
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse: one pair on a washing line started smoking.",
   "s": 17.541,
   "e": 20.426
  },
  {
   "text": "Farmers hung their pants by the fire to dry. Bang.",
   "s": 20.726,
   "e": 23.093
  },
  {
   "text": "Seventy years later, a historian's paper about it won an Ig Nobel Prize.",
   "s": 23.393,
   "e": 27.374
  },
  {
   "text": "Its title? \"Mr. Richard Buckley's Exploding Trousers.\"",
   "s": 27.674,
   "e": 30.33
  }
 ],
